<title>MEXICO: Mexico awards tariff-free barley import quotas.</title>
<headline>Mexico awards tariff-free barley import quotas.</headline>
<dateline>MEXICO CITY 1997-06-10</dateline>
<text>
<p>Mexico's Trade Ministry published on Tuesday its 1997 quotas for tariff-free imports of roasted malting barley for use mainly by the beer industry.</p>
<p>In the government's Official Gazettee, the ministry said it had awarded the United States a duty-free quota of 138,915 tonnes and Canada a quota of 34,729 tonnes.</p>
<p>The quotas were awarded under terms of the North American Free Trade Agreement (NAFTA). It gave quotas for unroasted malting barley.</p>
<p>The ministry said that this year a system of direct allocation of import quotas would be applied to &quot;promote the (competitiveness) of the beer industry&quot;.</p>
<p>Beer makers and other food industries using roasted malting barley can apply for the quotas, the ministry said. It said the total import quota amount for non-beer industries was capped at 28,000 tonnes.</p>
<p>((--Chris Aspin, Mexico City newsroom +525 7289530))</p>
